    "created_at": "2026-02-27T19:17:59Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-02-27T19:22:09Z",
    "body": "For Chromium specifically we blocked these methods when the top-level origin is opaque because our UI can't render the string \"&lt;origin> wants to connect to a serial port\" because our implementation renders an opaque origin as the empty string. Note that this only applies to opaque top-level origins because that is the origin displayed to the user. Normal sandboxed iframes are fine.\n\nFrom a web security model perspective I don't think there's anything wrong with an opaque origin requesting a permission as long as that can be communicated to the user in a meaningful way. Of course that permission will always be ephemeral because opaque origins always compare as unequal.\n\nIt's possible that it would be valid to use the precursor origin when displaying an opaque origin in this context and Chromium could remove this restriction, but I'd have to check with our security experts to make sure that makes sense. ",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
